  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe first signs of fall as temperatures as of mid-afternoon were\nin the middle to upper 60s under partly cloudy skies. Gusty \nnorthwest of 10 to 20 mph were noted with gusts up to 25 mph.\nDewpoint readings across the north have fallen into the middle \nto upper 30s.\n\nFor tonight, high pressure will slide eastward and will be \ncentered across northeast WI overnight. The combination of clear \nskies and light winds will allow temperatures to plummet\ninto the 30s over northern WI and in some sections of central\nWI. A few our our typical cold spots may drop to or a few\ndegrees below freezing for a couple of hours around sunrise.  \nExpanded the Frost Advisory to include Langlade, and northern \nsections of Marinette and Oconto counties with the afternoon \nissuance. \n\nAttention turns to the next system that will bring the next\nround of rain and a few thunderstorms. Latest model trends \nwould suggest rain would be a few hours slower arriving across\nfar northeast WI and probably would not occur until early evening.\nThe risk of severe weather is very low with models indicating the\nmost unstable CAPE values of 50-150 J/KG across central into\nnortheast WI Monday evening. The NBM was highlighting a 60-90% \nchance of 1 inch or more of rain from Marshfield to Wausau\nto Merrill northeast into Marinette County. The probabilities drop\noff sharply to 15 to 30% from Green Bay and Appleton and points \nto the southeast and east. The NBM also showed a 50-70% chance \nof 2+\" of rain from Wausau to Marshfield. The LREF probabilities \nwere somewhat lower, but were still highlighting the heaviest\nrainfall out towards Wausau. Any rain across northeast WI will\ncome to an end Tuesday morning.\n\nHigh pressure will build into the region on Tuesday, although\ngusty winds will continue to bring some hazardous conditions\non the bay and lake. The previous forementioned baroclinic zone\nis expected to set up south of the area for the rest of the week.\nA wave moving along the boundary could bring a chance of \nrain Wednesday afternoon into Wednesday night, although \nconfidence is low on the northward extent of the precipitation\nfield. The models are in an agreement that feature moving across\nthe area Friday night into Saturday will likely be the highest\nchances/greatest confidence in the chances for rain during the \nperiod. Temperatures for much of the period will be at or slightly\nabove normal.",
